{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310696,"nonce":"38pL4NkgGWiyZKslLO5Z7rOy2q8=","title":"2026-10-05/06 d6e766d6, 3ee1841f, Int64-shadow commit (837-\u003e839 on nuc): symlinked binary lost haxe-std on Linux; optional abstract params; ...","message":"**Symbol.qualified_name is NOT a package path for type symbols.** `update_symbol_qualified_name` rebuilds it from the SCOPE chain, and types live in the root scope, so `haxe.Int64`, `haxe.Int32`, `rayzor.Ptr`, `rayzor.Usize` all carry the BARE name. (Private helpers like `haxe.__Int64` do get `haxe.`.) The user asked \"why not use qualified names\" — right instinct, but check what the field holds before keying on it; I shipped a dead `\"rayzor.Usize\"` match in 3ee1841f and only caught it by printing. Reliable identities: `package_id` (TAST only — MIR has no namespace resolver), `native_name` (`@:native(\"rayzor::Ptr\")`, verbatim), or a qn that genuinely contains a package.\n\n**Bare-name binding layers a user `Int64` passed through (451a8a3):**\n1. Pre-registration reused the root `Int64` slot — abstracts lacked the class path's `root_slot_is_foreign_type` split. Foreign must mean \"root symbol has a DIFFERENT package\"; package `None` = a top-level placeholder to claim (treating None as foreign split haxe.Int64 ITSELF into two symbols and broke all Int64).\n2. `resolve_symbol_in_scope_hierarchy` fallback took the root slot — `prefer_own_module_type` (current_module_types).\n3. MIR `try_static_receiver_call` mapped the call through the haxe.Int64 RUNTIME MAPPING by receiver name — skip when the method symbol's owner has no `class_key`.\n4. **The stdlib MERGE (driver.rs) replaces ANY user function whose bare name matches a retained stdlib function** — a user's own `make` body was swapped for haxe's. Now protected when bodied and owned by another type (qn compared up to qualification).\n\n**Traps:** the gate's fixture line is `FAIL \u003cname\u003e`, not `FAILED` — my monitor grep missed DynamicHeldValues failing in three gates (2b0ca41, pre-existing on Linux). macOS sed has no `\\b` (use perl). The final post-merge MIR dump hides which layer rebound a call — instrument TAST (method symbol), HIR (field symbol), MIR (func_id) in turn. Issue3214 itself is now blocked on unboxed Int-\u003eDynamic arithmetic (6175b3e), same at HEAD with the type renamed. `f.set(B)` on a local EnumFlags doesn't write `this` back (42d28bb).\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): 2026-10-06 (nuc 837-\u003e839): a TYPE's qualified_name is usually its BARE name (scope-built) — key on package_id/native_name. User `Int64` merged with haxe's at 4 layers incl. the stdlib MERGE replacing user fns by bare name. Gate fixture lines are `FAIL x`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_shadowed_stdlib_names_and_qualified_names` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}